 # Angular Spectrum Method (ASM)
 
-Also called: Free-space propagation operator
+Also called: Fourier Optics Propagation, Free-space propagation operator, Helmholtz equation solution in Fourier space
 
 ## Overview
-The Angular Spectrum Method (ASM) is a free-space propagation operator used to model the parallel propagation of a wave field from one plane to another without approximations [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of accurately reconstructing or propagating a complex optical field $U_0(x_0, y_0)$ to a target plane at distance $z$ by treating the field as a superposition of plane waves [S1]. Unlike the Fresnel Method, which relies on paraxial approximations, ASM calculates the wavefield by propagating plane waves in different directions, making it exact for any propagation distance and angle, provided the sampling conditions are met [S1].
+The Angular Spectrum Method (ASM) is a free-space propagation operator used to model the parallel propagation of a wave field from one plane to another [S3]. It solves the problem of accurately reconstructing or propagating a complex optical field $U_0(x_0, y_0)$ to a target plane at distance $z$ by treating the field as a superposition of plane waves propagating in different directions [S1]. Unlike the Fresnel Method, which relies on paraxial approximations, ASM operates in the frequency domain to solve the Helmholtz equation without approximations, avoiding the computational cost of first-principles methods like FDTD [S2, unofficial].
 
